 and you can see who  it's being targeted at and actually with  the only platform that actually offers  that sort of transparency if you want to  there's no way that you can go and see  what the leaflets are being put through  people's doors or or what adverts were  running in the press or even in posters  even last week so all this now exists on  in Facebook and will be there for seven  years so you can constantly go back and  check and see what people are doing so  I'm proud of that I'm proud that you  know that we're doing that but you know  there's always more that we can be doing  and we're always iterating on our  platforms as well so I'm pleased with  the progress we're making there oh but  what about the fact that a political  party could go on and put a video that  has outright lies I think you know news  outlets for example Sky News is under  more scrutiny than ever before for  accountability fact checking balance and  it seems that politicians party is on  Facebook can say whatever they want  uncheck it again and I think people have  a  problem with that if you see something  on Facebook how do you know that it's  true so if there's anything I'll know  that could cause real-world harm or  could impact in any way the you know  people going out to vote that would come  down that would have no place on our  platform want to be really clear about  that if something has been labeled in  the past as if as misinformation that  couldn't be used in advertising but  other than that yes it could be out  there and then part of that is what we  see happens anyway in the natural course  of an election is that people will  discuss it people will debate it and  then what it does is allows the public  to form proper full opinions of the  different messages that different  political parties are putting out there  deep fakes is something I actually  learned about first on Facebook and it  was a clip of a Saturday night talk show  where comedians face had been replaced  with Tom Cruise's face and the  technology was such that you couldn't  notice the transition and that's a real  concern now deep face not just because  of you know light-hearted comedy moments  but in terms of you know putting words  in the mouths of people you know that  aren't true what a face book doing to  challenge deep faiths because of course  that technology is extremely advanced  and extremely new so one of the things  that we we're doing a lot more of is the  investment that we're making into  machine learning and artificial  intelligence and it is almost like if  you like an arms race against the bad  guys that you know will bring in some  tools and they'll break you know they'll  move again and so we will as well  we've also traveled the number of people  that are working in this area around  safety on our platforms to 35,000 people  over the last couple of years as well  and the total summer monies that we're  spending in this whole area to combat  these sorts of things with  misinformation false news is actually  more than the total revenues of the  business of Facebook when we went to IPO  so that's billions and billions of  dollars but we're not going to be sad as  we know we've got to keep investing here  because we know these things were  important talk about Facebook learning  and evolving do you think Mark  Zuckerberg will ever change his stance  on political advertising because it is  such a tricky issue in terms of you know  people talk about free speech and people  being able to make up their own minds  but it's so nuanced a lot of political  advertising if it's not outright lies  it's forming people's opinion and  algorithms and the weight gets to  somebody is so complicated do you think  that he would change his stance in terms  of not an outright ban but have more  stricter rules in the future I think  where we are is where we are at the  moment and what I see though and the  reason it really matters and it's  important is because it also gives voice  to the smaller political parties that  are trying to break through as well you  know the big parties they've got voice  they've got the reach but actually the  smaller parties and especially you know  in the election that we're seeing at the  moment I think people are very  interested and so you can't just band  for one and not the other that's why we  want to have that platform to allow all  the parties to talk that's just a  snapshot of the huge pressures you face  at work and at home we've talked about  that what's next for you as we said  you're in remission you're doing well  busy at work what's next for Nicola  Mendelsohn well the I've never really  looked at things like that the way that  I that I think about things is am I  still learning am i enjoying what I'm  doing and then well then I'm a lucky  person well Nicola he certainly has been  really interesting to talk to you learn  more about follicular lymphoma good luck  with the foundation thank you for  talking to us about and the many issues  associated with Facebook at the moment  it's always a pleasure to talk to you  thank you
 

